e is that it can all be tracked  it can be followed and unless you put  privacy safeguards in place it can be  highly monitored but we don't have to  use these programs that use ones and  zeros we don't have to use you know  Facebook we don't have to use doing it  well we don't have to I mean at this  point i would say that facebook is  basically a public utility you look  you've got over a billion people using  this service that's that's not a choice  for many people certainly not choice for  a lot of teenagers it's kind of social  suicide to not be a part of that you can  be rebel but I I don't know so at this  point it's hard to say that that opting  out is completely an option this new law  that I signed today will allow  surveillance of all communications used  by terrorists including emails the  internet and cell phones the Patriot Act  is they may be a central character and  you're in your film can you talk about  the patriot act and how it changed  google's privacy policy before and after  911 well what i do in the film ie I go  back and I look at this independent  archive service the Wayback Machine and  I I compare google's privacy policy from  before nine eleven and then after nine  eleven and there were significant  changes the thing is those changes are  only apparent if you use the time  machine they're not apparent if you go  to google's archive service in fact what  Google has done is they've covered up  their original privacy policy you can't  find it on their archives page and it's  hard to know exactly why but I can  surmise why because the one that they  are not showing the privacy policy that  they are not putting on their page  basically says that users are considered  anonymous that they will not share your  data unless you've expressly given them  permission to and then after nine eleven  this changed and and the policy grows  and it talks about all of the different  ways in which data can be shared the  question of your if you will information  being retained by Google is not really  at this point at Google decision it's  really a political or public policy  decision enforced by different  governments in different ways what if  privacy policies weren't about  protecting privacy at all but rather  taking it away one of my favorite  moments in the film is when you go and  meet with a man who had typed into  Google how to kill my wife multiple  times and you found out something when  you went to go meet him well yeah I mean  these so al had released this anonymized  search history and everybody on the  internet who had looked at  this series of searches was convinced  this guy was murder it was like how to  kill my wife wife kill it decapitated  bodies and then like in the middle he  searches for steak and cheese but this  is this is the list and uh so I go over  to this gentleman's house and I show him  this list of things and I asked him you  know have you searched for these things  before he goes down the list he's like  yeah I have searched for every single  one of these including steak and cheese  and watching pilgrim oh my god this  guy's a murderer and then you realize I  asked well what were you doing I'm a  writer on cold case it was a writer on a  TV show a crime a crime drama so of  course that's why he was looking for  this stuff his wife pops out into it's a  cute scene but what this really shows is  that context is so important because  when you look at these searches outside  of what he was doing you can assign him  as a murderer but when you look at them  within context ago of course he was  writing a screenplay so this is the  problem with these systems on the whole  is that I think they're very good at  limiting journalism monitoring  protesters I think they're much worse at  stopping terrorists attacks because  they're not plotting things on Facebook  is there some sort of relationship  between government and and these big  data companies that is a is a hand  holding relationship is that a I don't  know what it is it's it's it's a cozy  relationship where they're both getting  a lot out of it right it's not like the  government and these companies got  together and said yeah let's let you  know it was more like mutually  beneficial so you have the company's  making a fortune and off of our data and  then you have the government getting  something that it's wanted for a very  long time which is this sort of  unprecedented access to all of the  information of all of its citizens but  Facebook can't put you in jail it can't  give you the death penalty at cancer no  not yet yeah facebook is going to give  you the death penalty but I mean they  don't have the power to do those sorts  of things but the government does right  right I mean that's the that's the  challenge here is because we soon as you  hand over your information to a third  party they can give it to the government  and the government can  I decide what they want to do with that  information the most gripping part of  the whole documentary is you confronting  Mark Zuckerberg outside of his home with  wearing a google glass and ended  following him with a camera why did you  decide to do it what did you get from it  what did you hope to get from this  interaction I had tried for four months  to get through to anyone at Facebook i  just wanted to discuss the privacy  practices of their company it didn't  need to be with Mark Zuckerberg and I'd  sent multiple emails the only email  address that they allow you to send  anything to and I got limited to no  response so i went into their office and  I said hey is there somebody here that  maybe I can talk to about this stuff and  they said send says it's an email they  don't even have a phone number you can  call they will take all of the phone  numbers from your phone but they don't  even have a phone number so there's this  real problem with transparency it's  one-sided transparency they want you to  be transparent they're not transparent  at 